@node Advanced Timely
@section Scopes, iteration and capture

The advanced features look like @emph{control flow}: dataflow patterns
that reshape how a computation runs, using progress information to
change what work happens.

@subsection Scopes

A scope is a region of dataflow, like a block in an imperative language.
@code{dtfl_scope_scoped} creates a nested scope; what a scope buys you is
the chance to @emph{enrich} the timestamp of the streams brought into
it.  An outer scope might carry a @code{u64} time; a nested scope can
augment it to a product of @code{u64} and an iteration counter
(@pxref{Concepts}), so operators inside react to the richer time without
troubling the operators outside.

A stream must be carried across a scope boundary explicitly, because the
two scopes have different time types.  @code{dtfl_stream_enter} brings a
parent stream into a child scope; @code{dtfl_stream_leave} returns a
child stream to its parent.  @code{dtfl_scope_region} makes a child with
the same time class (a grouping and logging boundary that needs no time
transform); @code{dtfl_scope_iterative} makes a child whose inner time
is a @code{u64} loop counter.

@subsection Iteration

Iteration is the signature advanced feature: because progress tracking
follows timestamps around cycles, a dataflow can contain a loop,
circulate data within it, and still let downstream operators judge their
input's progress.

The tool is a @dfn{feedback} stream, whose contents are not yet defined
when you start using it.  @code{dtfl_feedback_new} creates one, taking a
path summary that says how much to advance the time each time around the
loop (@pxref{Concepts}); @code{dtfl_feedback_connect} later binds its
contents.  As a worked example, the Collatz conjecture: divide an even
number by two, triple an odd number and add one, and every start
eventually reaches one.

@example
dtfl_summary_default(dtfl_scope_time_class(scope), summary);
summary[0] = 1;                 /* advance the loop coordinate by one */
cycle = dtfl_feedback_new(scope, dtfl_type_u64(), summary, &handle);
step = dtfl_stream_concat(in, cycle);
step = dtfl_stream_flat_map(step, dtfl_type_u64(), collatz, DTFL_NOCTX);
step = dtfl_stream_inspect(step, show, DTFL_NOCTX);
step = dtfl_stream_filter(step, greater_than_one, DTFL_NOCTX);
dtfl_feedback_connect(handle, step);
@end example

The input is concatenated with the feedback stream, so the Collatz step
applies both to the start value and to everything that comes back around
the loop; values that reach one are filtered out, and the survivors feed
back.  The complete program is in @file{doc/snippets/timely_iterate.c};
starting from nine, it prints the 19 steps down to one.  A program may
hold as many feedback streams as it likes, which is how you express
mutual recursion (an even loop and an odd loop, say).

@subsection Flow control

Flow control is about @emph{when} data flow, not where.  A stream that
expands each input into many outputs (every number less than it, say)
can produce far more data than it consumes, which may overwhelm a
downstream operator.  @code{dtfl_iterator_source} is the flow-controlled
source: it emits records with times the program chooses, holding back
while a probe shows the computation is behind, so the volume in flight
stays bounded.

@subsection Capture and replay

@code{dtfl_stream_capture_into} records everything about a stream: the
data that arrive and every change in the frontier.  The record is a
sequence of events delivered to an event pusher.  An event link
(@code{dtfl_event_link_new}) is an in-memory log of those events; its
synchronized form may be pushed on one thread and replayed on another.
@code{dtfl_event_link_replay} re-introduces a captured stream into a new
dataflow, and @code{dtfl_event_link_extract} collects the captured
@code{(time, record)} pairs after the computation finishes, sorted,
which is how a test asserts a stream's contents.  For transport beyond
one process, @code{dtfl_event_writer_new} and
@code{dtfl_event_reader_replay} write and read the events on a file
descriptor.

@subsection Custom datatypes

Rust lets an operator carry any @code{Clone} type, and exchange any type
that also serializes.  libdtfl has no type parameters: a record is a
byte string with a @code{dtfl_type} descriptor (@pxref{Records}).  A
custom datatype is therefore a custom descriptor: supply @code{cmp},
@code{hash} and @code{fmt} callbacks (or rely on the defaults) and wrap
it with @code{dtfl_type_new}.  Because records are already bytes,
exchange is a @code{memcpy}; there is no separate serialization step and
no @code{ExchangeData} bound to satisfy.
